# How to Show Progress Bar While Loading Using AJAX

When a user selects an item from a dropdown menu like `#client`, your application often needs to pause briefly to fetch data from the server. During that gap, the interface can feel unresponsive or frozen if you don't provide visual feedback. While there are many tutorials focused on file uploads where progress bars naturally map to bytes sent, those approaches rarely apply to simple data requests triggered by DOM events. The core challenge here is decoupling the visual indicator from the actual payload size, because you likely don't have access to a `progress` event for standard POST requests that just return JSON or HTML strings.

To implement this effectively in your existing jQuery setup, you need to manage the lifecycle of the progress bar manually within the AJAX call itself. The most robust pattern involves hiding the progress bar before the request starts and showing it immediately after. This ensures the UI reflects the state accurately without relying on browser timing quirks or server-side callbacks that might not fire in your specific environment.

Here is how you can refactor your current code to handle this state management cleanly:

```javascript
$(function() {
    $("#client").on("change", function() {
        var clientid = $("#client").val();

        // Hide the progress bar initially if it exists
        $("#loading-progress").addClass("hidden");

        $.ajax({
            type: "post",
            url: "clientnetworkpricelist/yourfile.php",
            data: { title: clientid }, // Note: Use object syntax for cleaner data handling
            success: function(data) {
                $("#result").html(data);

                // Ensure the progress bar is hidden after completion
                $("#loading-progress").addClass("hidden");
            },
            error: function(xhr, status, error) {
                // Handle errors gracefully
                console.error(error);

                // Hide progress bar on failure to avoid confusion
                $("#loading-progress").addClass("hidden");

                // Optionally show an error message in #result or a dedicated container
                $("#result").html("<p class='error'>Failed to load data.</p>");
            },
            complete: function() {
                // This fires on both success and error
                // Logic to hide the bar can go here, but explicit handling in callbacks is safer
            }
        });
    });
});
```

In this implementation, the decision to use the `complete` callback or explicit calls within `success` and `error` depends on your specific HTML structure. If your progress bar element has a CSS class named `.hidden` that sets `display: none`, toggling that class at the start and end of the operation provides immediate feedback.

A common pitfall with this approach is leaving the progress bar visible if the server returns an error or times out. In the code above, we explicitly hide the element in both the `success` and `error` blocks to prevent the UI from showing a loading spinner after the operation has finished, whether successfully or not. This is critical for accessibility and user experience; users shouldn't see a spinner when content has already loaded or failed.

If you are using modern frameworks like Laravel, consider leveraging the built-in `response()->stream()` method if your backend can simulate chunks of data being sent. However, for standard PHP scripts returning a single string or JSON object, manual state control via jQuery callbacks remains the most straightforward solution without overcomplicating the backend logic.

Remember to ensure your CSS handles the transition smoothly. A simple fade effect using `transition: opacity 0.3s` on the progress bar element can make the start and stop actions feel less jarring than an instant appearance or disappearance. Always verify that the progress bar is removed from the DOM or hidden properly if you are dynamically appending content to it later, to prevent memory leaks in long-running applications.
